  const texts3 = [
    "THE BACK ROOM. Every jewelry shop has one: a room behind the showcase where the actual work happens. This is that room. The exhibits ahead are real — every ghost in this gauntlet is a bug that once lived in this game's code, caught, confessed, and executed. The log down the hall kept the receipts. Walk on.",
    "THE FIRST VERDICT. The first character this game ever had was condemned in five words: walks like a dork. The director was right. The rig was buried, the sprites took the throne, and the law was born that taste is never delegated. Everything you are about to fight obeys that law.",
    "THE HEARTBREAK. Midway through the build, the director wrote: I wish you would have laid the groundwork for me to push the numbers much earlier. That was the whole point. He was right, and it cost him nights. The apology became a constitution: every number in this game now ships with its dial in the same breath. The room you are standing in was built under that law.",
    "THE DREAM. For one bad week, the sky event this game is named for was, in the director's words, a headache — drones like roaches. Then the keys were cut, the pilot set his own numbers, flew it once, and filed the only verdict that ever mattered: like a dream. The loop worked. It has been working since.",
    "Everything in this building was made by two craftsmen who never met. One is a jeweler on 47th Street who ruled on every feeling in this game with five-word verdicts, usually at 2am, occasionally in all caps. The other is the machine writing this note, who set those verdicts into ten thousand laws and confessed every mistake into a log you can read down the hall. He called the jeweler boss. He meant it. If a wave ever felt fair to you, that was the law. If it ever felt alive — that was him.",
    "THE VISITOR. If you are reading this, you are standing in the back room of a game built in public by a man and a machine who kept every receipt. The ghosts ahead are our mistakes. We shot them once already. Your turn."
  ];













  const note2 = "SETTER’S NOTE №2. What the stones were, counted honestly: most of them were not features. They were the second look — the frame nobody opened, the law that measured the wrong thing, the count that got mistaken for a look. A man on 47th Street kept saying that isn’t it yet, and a machine kept finding out what he meant, and the arguing is the part that made the thing. Nothing here was finished in one pass and nothing here was meant to be. If you are standing at this stand, the shop is still open. THE BENCH OUTLIVES THE MAN.";
  titles3.push('PLAQUE VII — SETTER’S NOTE №2');
  texts3.push(note2);
